entry_id = "TEMP_M2_RESEARCH_PROFILE"
source_kind = 0
source_id = "TEMP_M2_PROFILE"
title = "Past records / observations versus interpretation"
body_text = "A folded, silver-grey membrane sac about 70 cm long. It has a dark longitudinal seam and many fine, dry fringes, but no visible eyes or mouth. The fringes rise before the body unrolls. It was recovered as a compact fold in a metal collection tray.\n\nThe following observations and interpretations belong only to this temporary playtest. They do not identify an official Case or establish facility history.\n\nOBSERVATION A — The recovery tray was connected to a grounding lead; the room meter read 61% relative humidity. Both conditions were present together, so the recovery does not identify which mattered. Moving the fold onto an acrylic packing mat preceded fringe lifting. The humidity during transfer was not recorded.\n\nOBSERVATION B — A warm metal tool pressed the fringes down while touching the seam. After contact ended they rose again. Tool temperature, conductivity and the contact itself were not separated.\n\nOBSERVATION C — Direct droplets loosened transparent membrane pieces. A subsequent air pulse carried pieces beyond the tray. The same report records no comparable shedding during humid-air exposure without droplets. No conclusion about immersion safety was established.\n\nWORKING INTERPRETATION — The earlier observer proposed that the sac seeks warm, moist surroundings. This is an interpretation, not a measured preference. The warm tool was also metal and made contact; humid air and liquid water were not equivalent in the observations.\n\nBefore choosing a configuration, separate attraction to a person from attraction to something the person carries. Compare electrical connection at the same material and temperature, then compare humidity without wetting the body. Avoid treating a reduction in one brief observation as proof of long-term containment. These notes leave the exact configuration open."

